body:
@CharliePoole If it helps, I have tried debugging what happens, and the filter that the NUnit framework reciewes is `<filter><not><or><not><test>Namespace.Fixture</test></not><cat>X</cat></or></not></filter>`.

I just tested, and it also happens with the filter `!(cat!='Y'|cat='X')` if the test is given the category `Y`.

Come to think of it, I think that it might actually be closely related to #3231. Indeed, as the `cat=='X'` should return `false` on both its `Pass` and `Match` methods for every test, the actual functionality of the `OrFilter` is simply to forwards its `Pass` and match `Match` methods to the inner `NotFilter`, which produces the same effect as if it was two `NotFilter`'s in a row.

I'm new to the NUnit source, so I might have misunderstood something, but to me it seems like the problem is that the `Pass` method on filters inheriting from `TestFilter` searches through parent tests _and_ child tests in order to check whether there is a match, whereas the `NotFilter` only searches upwards through parent tests (as it calls the `Match` method and not the `Pass` method of the base filter).

I tried writing a test for it. So, with the possibility that I have misunderstood how the tests work, I wrote the following three tests with inspiration from `DoubleNegativeFilterTests`:
```
public class NotOrNotFilterTests : TestFilterTests
{
    private readonly TestFilter _filter;

    public NotOrNotFilterTests()
    {
        _filter = new NotFilter(new OrFilter(new NotFilter(new CategoryFilter("Dummy")), new CategoryFilter("NoExistingCategory")));
    }

    [Test]
    public void PassToplevel()
    {
        Assert.That(_filter.Pass(_topLevelSuite));
    }

    [Test]
    public void PassFixture()
    {
        Assert.That(_filter.Pass(_fixtureWithMultipleTests));
    }

    [Test]
    public void MatchTest()
    {
        Assert.That(_filter.Match(_fixtureWithMultipleTests.Tests[1]));
    }
}
```
The first two, `PassToplevel` and `PassFixture`, fail but the last one, `MatchTest`, succeeds. This seems like a mistake to me, and if I understand how the NUnit framework goes through the tests, this means that it simply skips namespaces/fixtures which should not have been skipped.
